(ii) Given, mass of nitrogen $(m)=14 \mathrm{~g}$<br/>Molar mass of $\mathrm{N}_2(w)=28 \mathrm{~g}$
Number of moles of $\mathrm{N}_2=\frac{m}{w}=\frac{14}{28}=\frac{1}{2}=0.5 \mathrm{~mole}$<br/>Volume occupied by 1 mole of $\mathrm{N}_2$ at STP
$=22.4 \mathrm{~L}$<br/>Volume occupied by 0.5 mole of $\mathrm{N}_2$ at STP
$\begin{aligned}
& =0.5 \times 22.4 \\
& =11.2 \mathrm{~L}
\end{aligned}$
(iii) Given, number of molecules of $\mathrm{O}_2=10^{21}$<br/>Number of molecules of $\mathrm{O}_2$ in 1 mole
$\begin{aligned}
& =6.022 \times 10^{23} \text { molecules } \\
\text { Number of moles } & =\frac{10^{21}}{6.022 \times 10^{23}} \\
& =0.166 \times 10^{-2} \text { moles } ;
\end{aligned}$<br/>Volume occupied by $0.166 \times 10^{-2}$ mole of
$\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{O}_2 & =0.166 \times 10^{-2} \times 22.4 \mathrm{~L} \\
& =3 . \nVdash 2 \times 10^{-2} \\
& =0.0372 \mathrm{~L}
\end{aligned}$<br/>Therefore, correct increasing order of volume occupied by the given amount of molecules at STP is : (iii)  &lt; (ii) &lt; (i).

