Given that, $f(x)=\sqrt{1+x^2}\begin{array}{ll}
\therefore & f(y)=\sqrt{1+y^2} \\
\text { and } & f(x y)=\sqrt{1+x^2 y^2}
\end{array} \\ Now, $\quad \| f(x y)=f(x) f(y)\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow \quad \sqrt{1+x^2 y^2} & =\sqrt{1+x^2} \sqrt{1+y^2} \\
& =\sqrt{\left(1+x^2\right)\left(1+y^2\right)}
\end{aligned}$ \\ On squaring both sides, we get
$\begin{aligned}
& 1+x^2 y^2=\left(1+x^2\right)\left(1+y^2\right) \\
\Rightarrow \quad & 1+x^2 y^2=1+y^2+x^2+x^2 y^2
\end{aligned}\begin{aligned} & \therefore \quad 1+x^2 y^2 \leq 1+x^2+y^2+x^2 y^2 \\ & \text { Hence, } \quad f(x y) \leq f(x) \cdot f(y)\end{aligned}

$E=E_0 \cos (k x-\omega t)\therefore \quad \omega=2 \pi \times 6 \times 10^{14}\begin{array}{ll}
