& & e V_s & =2.48-1.5=0.98 \mathrm{eV} \\
& V_s & =0.98 \mathrm{~V}
\end{array}

The electric potential due to an electric dipole at a distance $r$ from its centre is given by
$\begin{aligned}
& { } 1 \mathrm{~V}=\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_0} \cdot \frac{p \cos \theta}{r^2} \\
& \text { [where, } p=\text { electric dipole moment] }
\end{aligned}\therefore \quad V \propto \frac{1}{r^2}$

By Faraday's law of electromagnetic induction,
$\begin{array}{ll}
\varepsilon=\frac{d \phi}{d t}, \text { Also, } \varepsilon=i R \\
\therefore & \frac{d \phi}{d t}=i R \Rightarrow d \phi=i R d t \\
\Rightarrow & \int d \phi=R \int i d t
\end{array}
$\therefore \quad \phi=R \times$ Area of current versus time
